metrics-server scrapes the kubelet, so `kubectl top` and HorizontalPodAutoscaler
work. It talks to the kubelet over TLS with a certificate the kubelet signed for
itself, which no public CA vouches for, so --kubelet-insecure-tls is set. Every
local-cluster distribution does the same thing for the same reason.

  kubectl top pods -A
  kubectl top nodes

It has nothing to report for its first fifteen seconds or so, which is why
enabling it waits until `kubectl top nodes` answers.

This used to come with a warning that the aggregated API could not be reached
unless ClusterIPs were bound on the Mac with FERRY_HOST_CLUSTER_IPS=1. That is
no longer true: the Mac is on the pod network now, so the API server reaches
metrics-server the same way it would on any other cluster, with no root and no
extra flag.
